{{also|bohemia|Bohémia|Bohèmia}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Latinized translation of {{der|en|fr|Bohème}}, from {{der|en|LL.|Boiohaemum}}, compound of {{m|la|Boii|Boio-|the Boii}}, the {{der|en|cel|-}} tribe previously inhabiting the area and {{der|en|gem|-}} {{m|gem-pro|*haimaz||home}} (more at {{l|en|home}}). The endonym is from {{der|en|cel-pro|*boyos}} and could ultimately be from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʷṓws||cattle}} (compare {{der|en|cel-pro|*bāus||cattle}}, genitive ''*bowos''), a reference to cattle owners, or from {{m|ine-pro|*bʰeyh₂-||to hit}}, i.e. “warrior, strong hitter” (compare {{der|en|cel-pro|*binati||to strike, hit}}).

Bohemia was abandoned by the Boii c. 60 BCE and settled by the Germanic [[w:Marcomanni|Marcomanni]] shortly thereafter.<ref>Dietz, Karlheinz (Würzburg). “Boiohaemum.” ''Brill’s New Pauly'', 2012. Reference. 14 March 2012 [http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/brill-s-new-pauly/boiohaemum-e218860]</ref> Related to {{l|en|Bavaria}}.

